WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.217 --> 00:00:01.670
(upbeat music)

00:00:01.670 --> 00:00:03.750
- This is Taylor, Texas.

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:06.250
And it might feel like we
are in the middle of nowhere

00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:07.940
but we're only about a half hour

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:11.670
outside of one of America's
biggest boom towns, Austin.

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:14.320
Taylor only has about 17,000 people

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:16.760
and the downtown has
shrunk in recent years

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:19.750
but that's about to change
because of a huge investment.

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:24.210
- Samsung has chosen
Taylor, Texas as the site

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:26.500
of his new state of the art

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:30.120
semiconductor chip fabrication plant.

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:32.170
- [Rob] Last fall, Samsung

00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:34.450
the world's largest semiconductor maker

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:35.310
announced that would build

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:40.310
a $17 billion chip fabrication
plant or fab in Taylor

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.770
creating more than 2,000 jobs.

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:45.990
And Samsung isn't alone,

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:47.830
Intel, Texas Instruments

00:00:47.830 --> 00:00:50.510
and TSMC are also returning

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:53.620
their chip manufacturing to the US.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:57.170
These companies were lured by
new state and local subsidies

00:00:57.170 --> 00:00:59.450
and they could soon
receive even more money

00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:01.090
from the federal government.

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:05.273
- If we'd be able to say made
in Ohio, made in America,

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:09.950
but we used to always be
able to say 25, 30 years ago.

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:11.400
That's what this is about.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:13.730
- [Rob] America has
long been losing ground

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:15.280
in the race to make chips.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:17.250
That's fueling bipartisan concerns

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:19.740
about our reliance on those made abroad.

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:22.240
And that's a problem
because semiconductors

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:24.920
are basically the
foundation of modern life.

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.110
They're in your car,
they're in your phone,

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:29.840
your laptop and even your washing machine.

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:31.280
But thanks to the pandemic,

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:33.560
chips have become harder to find.

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:37.640
The pandemic led to a spike
in global semiconductor sales

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:40.260
and that led to product
delays for consumers

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:42.350
and highlighted a growing problem.

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:44.660
America isn't anywhere near

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:47.060
making as many chips as it needs.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:50.000
I sat down with Samsung
Executive, Jon Taylor

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:51.750
at the company's Austin campus.

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:53.280
How big is that plant going to be?

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.230
- You know, we've got
1,200 acres in Taylor

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:58.990
so we're gonna construct 6
million square feet of buildings.

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:02.077
The capacity that we're
gonna bring online in Taylor

00:02:02.077 --> 00:02:03.570
is really gonna help us

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:05.700
give a lot of opportunity to our customers

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:07.380
and also help us to contribute

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:10.530
to the stability of the
semiconductor supply chain problem.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:11.630
- [Rob] To woo Samsung,

00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:13.480
local and state governments offered

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:15.770
a slew of financial incentives.

00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:18.000
The City of Taylor and Williamson County

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.560
offered 90% property tax
breaks for the first 10 years.

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:27.010
The State of Texas threw
in $27 million of its own.

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:28.540
In return, Samsung offered

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:30.440
to make donations to local schools

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:33.520
and guaranteed career
help for Taylor students.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:34.720
But some have questioned

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:37.160
if subsidies should even be necessary.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:42.160
- We are debating legislation
to provide some $53 billion,

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:49.060
billion dollars in corporate welfare

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:52.610
with no strings attached

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:56.580
to the highly profitable
micro chip industry.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:58.410
- [Rob] Samsung is one of the world's

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:00.230
most valuable companies,

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:02.983
it's worth around $388 billion.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:06.400
- So we're a few miles
from downtown Austin here.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:08.420
Downtown Austin is full of cranes,

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:11.140
big tech companies coming
that aren't getting subsidies.

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:12.010
They're bringing jobs here

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:14.140
and they're not asking
for anything in return.

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:15.840
Why do you need subsidies

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:17.610
in order to build this plant in Taylor?

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:19.590
If it's a great business
offer opportunity,

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:20.423
why not just build it?

00:03:20.423 --> 00:03:22.560
- I think the incentive
packages certainly lure

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:24.500
a lot of top companies here.

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:27.190
It makes it a competitive advantage

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:29.960
not only country to country,
but just within the states.

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:32.980
Texas and Williamson county and the city

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:35.930
aren't the only areas that are
offering incentive packages.

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:39.020
Certainly other places
that we look did the same.

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:41.200
Everybody would like to be able to get

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:43.850
these type of projects again in their area

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:46.090
'cause they can see the
economic development

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:46.923
that will happen

00:03:46.923 --> 00:03:49.210
and that's generational development too.

00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:50.370
This is not just short term,

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:52.320
it's really a long term investment.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:54.170
- [Rob] Samsung recently broke ground

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:55.170
on the Taylor site

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:56.670
and the company said it's fab

00:03:56.670 --> 00:03:59.830
would be up and running
by the end of 2024.

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:03.510
James Lewis is a technology
and public policy researcher

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:06.450
at CSIS, a DC think tank.

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:09.020
- Countries we have to
worry about are China.

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:10.590
- China, basically China.

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:13.480
- What happens to the American economy

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:16.710
or to the American consumers
ability to buy things

00:04:16.710 --> 00:04:19.590
if China decides that they
don't want Taiwan shipping

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:21.900
quite as many any chips to the US?

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:23.360
- If China were decide

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:26.613
to cut off supplies from
Taiwan of semiconductors,

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:29.470
it would cripple the global economy.

00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:31.990
- [Rob] In 1990, the
US and Europe produced

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:35.330
more than 75% of the world semiconductors.

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:37.670
Now, they produce less than a quarter.

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:41.060
Japan, South Korea, Taiwan, and China

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:42.670
are squeezing out the West.

00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:44.120
If those trends hold,

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:48.260
by 2030 China will become the
world's largest chip maker.

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:52.710
Aggravating matters is the
sheer thirst for chips in the US

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:54.970
even though domestic
production has fallen,

00:04:54.970 --> 00:04:58.070
demand for chips at home remain sky high.

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:01.550
As of 2019, nearly half
of all chips world wide

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:03.780
were bought by US companies.

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:06.440
- Are there any examples from history

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:08.470
of America ramping up production

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:10.000
of something like semiconductors

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:12.860
and being able to displace
some of those imports?

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:17.530
- You know, the idea that
we would use policy solution

00:05:17.530 --> 00:05:19.660
to increase production is not a new one

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:21.490
and it's industrial policy

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:23.680
which used to be a
dirty word in Washington

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:28.040
now you can say it railroads on airplanes,

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:31.180
spacecraft and semiconductors themselves.

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:33.580
All these grew out of
government investments.

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:36.060
- America invented these chips

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:38.070
and federal research and development

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:40.760
led to the creation of these chips.

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:42.360
Taxpayer dollars.

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:43.360
Over 30 years ago,

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:46.830
America had about 40%
of global production.

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:49.210
But since that time something happened.

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:51.360
American manufacturing,

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:54.350
the backbone of our
economy got hollowed out.

00:05:54.350 --> 00:05:55.190
- [Rob] In the past,

00:05:55.190 --> 00:05:58.890
the US didn't have federal
incentives for chip making.

00:05:58.890 --> 00:05:59.840
On the other hand,

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:03.040
Asian countries typically
offer cheap land to chip makers

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:05.820
and subsidized manufacturing equipment.

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:08.040
- That's one of the reasons that our share

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:10.520
of chip manufacturing has declined.

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:13.190
Everyone else subsidizes, we don't.

00:06:13.190 --> 00:06:15.800
The Chinese in particular
have been willing to dump

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:19.420
tens of billions of
dollars into subsidies.

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:21.220
And so everyone has figured out,

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:23.720
this is a strategic industry,

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:27.450
government can play a role by
accelerating investment in it.

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:29.470
- [Rob] The Biden
administration in Congress

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:32.040
have set in motion
legislation to provide funding

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:34.690
to stimulate investment in the US.

00:06:34.690 --> 00:06:38.140
In June, the Senate approved $52 billion

00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:41.720
in direct subsidies for new
semiconductor factories.

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:43.620
The house has yet to take action.

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:46.580
- Don't think of dollars,
think of aircraft carriers.

00:06:46.580 --> 00:06:47.800
You want a fab,

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:50.320
that's two or three
aircraft carriers, right?

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:53.170
We're talking huge investments
to build these things.

00:06:53.170 --> 00:06:55.120
- And by comparing it
to an aircraft carrier,

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:57.300
you're comparing it to national defense.

00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:59.400
You're saying this can
be as valuable for us

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:01.040
as another aircraft carrier.

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:02.970
- In fact, it's more valuable for us.

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:04.380
Chips are more valuable for us

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:05.770
than another aircraft carrier.

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:08.850
It's the chips industry
that is now underpinning

00:07:08.850 --> 00:07:10.270
the whole digital economy.

00:07:10.270 --> 00:07:11.960
- [Rob] In March, the CEOs

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:13.730
of major semiconductor companies

00:07:13.730 --> 00:07:17.300
testified at a Senate hearing
about the chip shortage.

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:21.010
They also encourage lawmakers
to pass the Chips Act.

00:07:21.010 --> 00:07:23.760
- In 1990, 80% of semiconductors

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:25.640
were built in US and Europe.

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:30.130
Today, 80% is in Asia
with only 12% in the US

00:07:30.130 --> 00:07:32.180
and half of that being Intel.

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:34.040
Asian countries moved aggressively

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:37.310
to attract this industry with
strong and center policies.

00:07:37.310 --> 00:07:39.830
Assuming we received
support from the Chips Act,

00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:41.780
the total investment
from the site could grow

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:45.100
to as much as 100 billion
over the next decade.

00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:47.360
We have also thrown our
factory doors open wide

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:51.270
to provide capacity for US
and global foundry companies.

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:55.200
We seek to rebuild the entire
supply chain on US soil.

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:56.480
- [Rob] One thing is for sure

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:59.050
the chip shortage is far from over.

00:07:59.050 --> 00:08:01.290
Last month, Intel Chief Gelsinger

00:08:01.290 --> 00:08:04.932
said the shortage would
extend into 2024 or beyond.

00:08:04.932 --> 00:08:07.515
(upbeat music)

